  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A widespread, soaking rainfall along with windy \nconditions are expected today, with unsettled weather lasting into \nthe weekend.\n\nPhasing of energy within the base of a negatively tilted trough \nacross the Ohio Valley is developing an upper level low overhead of \nthe Tennessee Valley, and consequently supporting a broad area of \nsurface low pressure along eastern North Carolina this morning, \nwhere this will continue its northward track today. This system is \nforecast to continue northward into the Northeast tonight and into \nFriday. Ahead of this system, deep moisture transport within an area \nof strong deformation on the northern flank of the aforementioned \nupper level low will support widespread precipitation to spread \nnorthward through the afternoon.\n\nCurrent radar imagery and surface observations are depicting the \nsteady rain approaching the NY/PA state lines. With a deep layer of \nlow level dry air in place, initial precipitation type could be a \nmix of rain and wet snowflakes due to wet bulbing, followed by a \ntransition to all rain as the warm nose strengthens. Precipitation \nwill gradually spread northward throughout the remainder of the \nafternoon and into the early evening. Speaking of which, the North \nCountry will likely remain dry through the afternoon and not see \nrain until the early evening. Temperatures aloft remain quite mild \nwith profiles suggesting almost all rain as the primary \nprecipitation type through Friday morning. A brief period of sleet \nis possible in higher elevations due to wet-bulbing, but this will \nquickly change to plain rain as precipitation continues.\n\nAdditionally, a strong easterly jet aloft and tight surface pressure \ngradient is bring windy conditions this afternoon through at least \nthe first half of tonight. Winds will likely remain just below below \nadvisory criteria, gusting to 35 to 45 mph, with some isolated gusts \n45+ mph possible over the lakeshore of Niagara and Orleans counties, \nand farther south downwind of the Chautauqua ridge.\n\nAs the mid-level closed low passes overhead Friday through Saturday, \nshowery conditions will persist. Additionally, the airmass overhead \nwill cool enough to support some lake enhancement as 850 mb \ntemperatures drop to -2C/-3C by late Friday. Winds will become \nwesterly Friday before shifting to southwesterly late Saturday into \nSaturday night. Showers will be likely east/northeast of the lakes \nFriday through Saturday. Model guidance continues to exhibit sounding \ndata to suggest plain rain across the lower elevations, whereas some \nwet snow may start to mix in across the higher terrain.  Marginal \nsurface temperatures and aloft suggest there will be little, if any \nsnow accumulation during this period, even across higher terrain. \nBest chance for a light coating would be across the higher terrain \nof the western Southern Tier.\n\nRainfall totals with this system remain in the 1.0\" to 1.5\" range, \nbut with much of the area in D1 moderate drought after the \nexceptionally dry conditions since September, this is expected to be \na beneficial rainfall with minimal hydro concerns at this time.",
